::Q1::Which approach is generally most appropriate when a knowledge set is large or changes frequently?
{
=Retrieval Augmented Generation (RAG)
~Cache Augmented Generation (CAG)
~Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning (PEFT)
~Putting all the context directly in the prompt
}

::Q2::What does an embedding represent in a RAG system?
{
~A copy of the original document stored in a database
~The complete model weights used for fine tuning
=A numeric representation of text that places similar meanings close together
~A prompt template that tells the model how to answer
}

::Q3::What is the key limitation of Cache Augmented Generation (CAG) compared with RAG?
{
~CAG requires a vector store for every question
~CAG cannot use a model's context window
~CAG requires retraining whenever a question is asked
=The entire knowledge set must fit within the model's context window
}

::Q4::What is Parameter Efficient Fine Tuning (PEFT) primarily intended to change?
{
~The set of documents retrieved for each question
=The model's behavior, such as its tone, format, or domain conventions
~The size of the model's context window
~The source documents stored in a vector store
}

::Q5::When diagnosing a poor answer from a RAG system, what should be checked first according to the module?
{
~Whether the model needs to be fully fine tuned
~Whether the context window should be made smaller
~Whether the prompt contains more instructions
=Whether the correct material was retrieved
}
